---
scope: src/tools/read
description: >
  Drill-in tool that returns a spec's raw content alongside its type, sibling specs, and extracted links so agents know what to read next from a single call.
intent: >
  aide_read is the drill-in tool — the one an agent calls after discover has
  pointed it at a specific spec. It returns the file's content with enough
  surrounding context (type classification, sibling specs in the same folder,
  extracted links) that the agent can decide what to read next without a
  second round-trip. The job of this tool is to make one read feel like the
  start of a trail, not the end of a lookup.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - A call with a valid path returns the spec's raw content verbatim,
      its classified type, the list of sibling .aide files in the same
      directory, and every link found in the body (wikilinks, relative
      paths, URLs).
    - Sibling discovery is scoped to the spec's own directory only. The
      agent learns what else lives alongside the spec without pulling in
      unrelated folders.
    - Links are extracted once, deduplicated, and returned as a flat list
      the agent can scan. Wikilinks, relative paths, and URLs are all
      treated as potential next reads.
    - A call with a missing path returns a readable "Path not found"
      message as the content, with type defaulted and siblings/links
      empty. The agent gets an actionable correction, not a thrown error.
  undesired:
    - A read that silently walks up to the project root gathering
      "related" specs. The sibling set is the immediate directory only;
      widening it burns context on files the agent did not ask for.
    - A read that re-renders the spec's body — stripping frontmatter,
      reformatting headers, or "summarizing" the content before returning
      it. The raw content is the contract. Any transform is a bug.
    - A read that throws on a missing file and leaves the agent with an
      opaque MCP error. Missing paths are an expected failure mode and
      must come back as text.
    - A link extractor that returns duplicates, partial matches, or
      fragments from inside code fences the agent cannot act on. The
      extracted list must be usable as-is.
---

## Context

Every other tool in this server either points the agent at a spec or
creates one; read is the only tool that transfers spec content into the
agent's working context. That makes it the load-bearing step between
"the agent knows a spec exists" and "the agent is using the spec to make
decisions". If read returns the content without the surrounding trail,
the agent has to make a second discover call to figure out what else is
nearby; if it returns the content transformed, the spec the agent is
reading is not the spec on disk.

## Strategy

**Return raw content, always.** The file's bytes are the contract. The
agent must see exactly what a human reading the file on disk would see —
frontmatter, section headers, body prose, everything. Any transform
(stripping YAML, rewriting headers, summarizing) breaks the agent's
ability to quote or reason about the spec verbatim, and the cost of
returning a few extra tokens is negligible against that.

**One read = one directory's context.** Siblings come from the spec's
own directory, not its ancestors, not its descendants. The agent asked
about this spec; the context it earns is what sits next to this spec.
Anything broader is a separate call.

**Links are a trail, not a graph.** Every link the spec contains is a
potential next read the agent might follow. The extractor pulls
wikilinks, relative paths, and URLs into a single deduplicated list and
returns them alongside the content. The agent decides which to follow —
this tool only surfaces the options.

**Missing paths are text, not exceptions.** A path that doesn't resolve
comes back as content reading "Path not found: <path>" with an empty
siblings and links list. The agent can retry with a corrected path; no
MCP error is raised, because the only information the agent would get
from that error is that something went wrong, which it already knew.

## Good examples

An agent calls `aide_read` on `src/tools/discover/.aide` and receives
the file's full body, its type classified as `intent`, a siblings list
naming the other `.aide` files in `src/tools/discover/` (empty here, as
discover has no sibling research spec), and a links list containing
every wikilink and relative path found in the content. The agent reads
the body, sees a reference to `progressive-disclosure`, and decides its
next read without calling discover again.

An agent calls `aide_read` on a spec whose body references
`../scaffold/index.ts`. The links list contains that relative path, and
the agent can follow it directly to the orchestrator the spec governs.

## Bad examples

A read that strips the YAML frontmatter before returning the body. The
agent cannot see the spec's declared scope or intent, cannot cite the
outcomes list, and has no way to tell whether it is looking at an
intent spec or a research spec without a separate classification call.
Every piece of information the frontmatter encodes is now missing, and
the agent has to guess.

A read that returns siblings from two directories up because "that's
the surrounding module". The agent asked about one file; it now has
eight files worth of sibling context it did not request, and the
tokens spent are tokens the actual body no longer has room for.

A read on a typo'd path that throws and reaches the agent as
`unexpected MCP error`. The agent cannot tell whether the spec is
missing, the path is wrong, or the server is broken, and has nothing to
retry from. The correct response is a single line of text naming the
missing path.
